# shellcheck shell=bash
# direnv configuration for pse-arrow.
#
# Contract: entering this directory is FAST and touches NO network. This file exports
# variables, puts the project's own tools first on PATH, and reports what is missing. It
# never creates a venv, installs a package, or downloads anything -- `just bootstrap` does
# that, deliberately and visibly.
#
# First time here:  direnv allow
#
# The environment itself comes from scripts/pse_env.py, the same boundary every recipe and
# agent command uses: the caller's values, then .envrc.local (personal, gitignored; see
# .envrc.local.example) for anything unset, then the repository defaults (PSE_PYTHON from
# .python-version, UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=.venv with its bin first on PATH, a local
# /opt/pse-solvers stack) and the compiler/cache configuration. Local values are rendered as
# deferred reads, so they never appear in direnv's or an agent's printed environment.
#
# Cargo's default target directory (<checkout>/target) is already per checkout, git worktrees
# included, so it is never exported: sccache keys every rustc call on its CARGO_* environment.
# Choose a different directory only with PSE_CARGO_TARGET_DIR.
eval "$(python3 scripts/pse_env.py --print)"

# Re-evaluate when any of these change, so a toolchain bump is picked up on the next cd.
watch_file .python-version
watch_file pyproject.toml
watch_file rust-toolchain.toml
watch_file .envrc.local
watch_file .config/build.toml
watch_file scripts/build_environment.py
watch_file scripts/pse_env.py

# Status block. Uses the system python deliberately: it has to work before the venv
# exists, which is precisely when you need to be told what to do.
if [ -f scripts/doctor.py ]; then
  python3 scripts/doctor.py --format=direnv || true
fi
